---
// /method — the measurement method, published.
//
// WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS: /loaners has been telling manufacturers "our measurement method is written
// down and published before anything gets measured, so you can read exactly how your headphone will
// be treated." It was written down (warren-labs/MEASUREMENT-PIPELINE.md) and it was not published.
// This page makes that sentence true. See warren-labs/PLAN.md item 6.
//
// WHY IT COULD BE PUBLISHED NOW: measurements/RIG.md used to say the protocol, the fixture and the
// tolerance math were "deliberately not published -- that is the moat, and it is also what makes the
// first call worth taking." That was written to protect the paid production-measurement service,
// which came down 2026-09-01. The thing the secrecy protected no longer exists.
//
// SOURCE: MEASUREMENT-PIPELINE.md sections 1-3 and the claimable block in measurements/RIG.md.
// Sections 4-8 of the pipeline (findings, data structures, script specs, open questions) stay
// internal -- they are not what anyone means by "the method."
//
// DELIBERATELY NOT HERE: the competitive positioning from pipeline section 1 (how we talk about
// GraphWorks), and any number the source flags as unverified. The 60318-4 defined range is
// described rather than pinned to a figure, because MEASUREMENT-PIPELINE.md section 2 says to
// verify the exact number against the standard before publishing it. Pin it and this page can say it.
//
// VOICE: checked against makerphones/scripts/voice-lint.mjs. Plain, first-person-plural, short
// sentences. No "X, not Y" as a structural device, no aphoristic closers, no lab-coat register.
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';

const SITE = 'https://warrenlabs.com';
const description =
  'How Warren Labs measures a headphone: the rig, the seating rule, the 19 captures per side, and ' +
  'the repeatability figures that came off our own bench. Published in full so you can check it.';

const RIG = [
  ['Fixture', 'miniDSP EARS Pro, both capsules, per-unit calibration at 1/24 octave.'],
  ['Coupler', 'IEC 60318-4 — the ear-simulator standard the industry rigs use.'],
  ['Analog gain', '0 dB, and it stays there. Moving it invalidates the calibration files.'],
  ['Sample rate', '48 kHz.'],
  ['Drive', 'The fixture&rsquo;s own headphone output, set once at 94 dB SPL and never touched between left and right.'],
  ['Captures', '19 per side. 38 per headphone. 60&ndash;90 minutes.'],
  ['Published band', 'Full. Nothing truncated at 8 kHz.'],
];

const BANDS = [
  ['20&ndash;200 Hz', 'Seal. How forgiving the headphone is about fit.'],
  ['200 Hz&ndash;2 kHz', 'The control band. Scatter here should be near zero. If it isn&rsquo;t, our measurement is wrong and we go again.'],
  ['2&ndash;10 kHz', 'Placement on the pinna. How fussy the headphone is about alignment.'],
  ['Above 10 kHz', 'Reported, but read it gently. See below.'],
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${SITE}/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'How we measure', item: `${SITE}/method` },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout title="How we measure · Warren Labs" description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Nav variant="home" />

  <main class="wrap mt">
    <nav class="crumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><span aria-current="page">How we measure</span>
    </nav>

    <span class="eyebrow">The bench</span>
    <h1>How we measure a headphone.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Every graph on this site came out of the procedure below. We are publishing it in full &mdash;
      the rig, the seating rule, the capture count, and the repeatability numbers off our own bench &mdash;
      so you can decide what our measurements are worth instead of taking our word for it.
    </p>

    <div class="terms">
      {RIG.map(([k, v]) => (
        <div class="term">
          <span class="tk">{k}</span>
          <span class="tv" set:html={v} />
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2>We measure it seated well, then prove we can find that seating again</h2>
      <p>
        This is the choice everything else follows from, so it goes first.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>We seat the headphone the way you would.</b> Shift the cups, move the headband, settle the
        pads, listen, adjust again. Keep going until it seals properly and sounds right. Then measure
        it there, and prove we can come back to the same spot.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is a departure from the usual practice, and the departure is on purpose. The convention
        headphone reviewing settled on &mdash; five placements per side, deliberately randomised, then
        averaged &mdash; was designed to answer a different question: where does a careless user end up?
        That is a fair question. It is also one nobody asks about their own headphones, because nobody
        listens at a random seating. You fiddle until it sounds right, and then you listen for three
        hours at that position.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the response worth publishing is the one you converge on. Our grid of nine positions is a
        search for that spot rather than a survey of how bad the misses are.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2>We never average the reseats</h2>
      <p>
        Averaging has a real problem underneath it. A seal either holds or it breaks, so the reseats
        come out in two clumps. Average four good seals with one broken one and you publish a bass
        shelf that no listener will ever hear, then build a correction on top of it. The average of a
        two-humped distribution is a value that never happened.
      </p>
      <p>
        What we publish instead is the <b>medoid</b>: of six real reseats at the winning coordinates,
        the one closest to the middle of the group. It is an actual placement that actually occurred,
        picked by a stated rule rather than by taste. Nothing is blended.
      </p>
      <p>
        The spread across those six gets published too. Nobody else publishes it, and it turns out to
        be the more interesting number.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2>The procedure, per side</h2>
      <p>
        <b>Fit.</b> Set the jig spacing for even pad pressure on both sides, write it down, and use
        that same setting every time we measure that model. Ever.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Nine on a grid.</b> A 3&times;3 pattern at a fixed step, with plate coordinates recorded for
        every position. Each one is normalised to 0 dB at 1 kHz and resampled to a log grid before
        anything is computed, so that a plain level shift between seatings doesn&rsquo;t read as
        instability and the top octave doesn&rsquo;t swamp the metric. The winner is the position that
        deviates least from its eight neighbours. If the winner lands on an edge of the grid, we
        guessed the starting point wrong, so we recentre and run it again.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Six reseats.</b> At the winning coordinates, lift the headphone off and seat it again, six
        times. The medoid of those six is the published response. Their spread is the repeatability
        figure, and if it comes out wide it means the coordinates aren&rsquo;t capturing the fit and
        the grid needs rerunning.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Four perturbations.</b> Headband too tight, headband too loose, and the seal broken with a
        thin eyeglass arm and a thick one. Glasses wreck a seal, and how much depends on the frame.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nineteen captures a side, thirty-eight a headphone. The sweeps themselves take seconds. All the
        time goes into moving the headphone.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2>Four bands, because they fail differently</h2>
      <div class="terms">
        {BANDS.map(([k, v]) => (
          <div class="term">
            <span class="tk" set:html={k} />
            <span class="tv" set:html={v} />
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="after">
        Spread is reported as a 10th&ndash;90th percentile range, which survives one bad seating and
        reads as a plain &plusmn; figure.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2>What our own bench does</h2>
      <p>
        We ran this on ourselves before we ran it on anyone&rsquo;s headphones, and the numbers are
        published here whether or not they flatter us.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>The instrument contributes 0.002 dB</b> through the midrange &mdash; thirty captures with
        nothing touched between them, both capsules agreeing. So the instrument is never the thing
        that limits a measurement here. Seating is.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>And seating repeatability belongs to the headphone.</b> That was the surprise. Measured
        across three headphones and five sides, seat-to-seat spread through the midrange ran from
        <b>0.059 dB on an HD 800</b> to <b>0.316 dB on an Oppo PM-3</b>. A five-fold range on one
        bench, one operator, one afternoon.
      </p>
      <p>
        We could quote you the 0.059 and let you assume it applies to everything. It would be a lie of
        the most convenient kind, and we have made a version of it before: this figure has been stated
        wrongly twice in our own notes, once too pessimistic and once too flattering. There is no
        single number that describes how repeatably this rig seats a headphone, because the headphone
        gets a vote.
      </p>
      <p>
        The two Sennheisers are opposites, which is the clearest way to see it. The HD 800 seals
        beautifully and hates being moved. The HD 650 is the reverse. So &ldquo;open circumaurals
        behave like this&rdquo; is not a rule, and no shortcut by category survives contact with the
        bench.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>The good news is that the hard ones are still measurable.</b> They need more seatings.
        Against our own derived tolerance, an HD 800 needs one seating per unit and a PM-3 needs eight.
        The method absorbs the difference by working harder, which is what a per-model tolerance is
        for.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2>Where our measurements stop being trustworthy</h2>
      <p>
        <b>Above roughly 10 kHz, read the graph gently.</b> The ear-simulator standard our coupler is
        built to has a defined range, and it ends up there. Past it, the coupler&rsquo;s own resonances
        start contributing rather than the eardrum pressure we are trying to represent. There is a
        second reason usually given &mdash; ear canals differ more between people than headphones differ
        from each other &mdash; and it is true, but softer. The instrument reason is the one that decides
        it.
      </p>
      <p>
        We publish that region anyway. Truncating at 8 kHz throws away pinna interaction and driver
        resonance, and that is most of what people mean when they talk about treble character. Better
        to show it and label it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>One unit is one unit.</b> A single measurement of a single sample is exactly that. Unit
        variation is real and we have no way to tell you where in the distribution yours fell.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Left and right are always measured separately.</b> Channels can differ by several dB. We
        keep both and never average them into one line.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Our repeatability figures are about our bench.</b> They describe how precisely we can
        repeat ourselves, on one rig, with one operator. They are not error bars on your headphone
        and they never appear as a band around a published curve.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2>Things we record that often go unrecorded</h2>
      <p>
        Pads dominate the response, and pad condition is the variable most commonly left out of a
        published measurement. So we write down the pad type, its age and condition, the jig spacing,
        and the clamping force if it has been modified. The headphone comes up to room temperature
        before it goes on the rig, because cold pads seal differently.
      </p>
      <p>
        The rig itself gets the same treatment. Capsule serial numbers, calibration files, gain
        setting, drive level. Every one of those is part of the measurement, and a change to any of
        them means measurements taken either side of it are not comparable &mdash; which we say out
        loud when it happens.
      </p>
      <p>
        One thing is still open and worth naming: the fixture&rsquo;s manual does not state the output
        impedance of its headphone output. A high output impedance interacts with a headphone&rsquo;s
        impedance curve and would put some of the amplifier into the result. We have asked. When we
        have an answer it goes here, and if it comes back high we will revisit the decision and say
        what changed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2>Two rigs never share an axis</h2>
      <p>
        We also hold an older archive measured on a Head Acoustics HMS II.3, from the HeadRoom years.
        It is a research reference and we treat it as one. It never shares a graph with anything
        measured here, and no count on this site ever adds the two together. Different fixture,
        different coordinate system, and no honest way to merge them.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-row tight">
        <Button href="/learn/why-two-rigs-cant-share-an-axis" variant="ghost">Why that matters</Button>
        <Button href="/learn/how-to-read-a-frequency-response-graph" variant="ghost">How to read the graph</Button>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="ln-cta">
      <div>
        <h2 class="cta-h">Send us a headphone and hold us to it.</h2>
        <p>
          Everything above is what happens to it. If we depart from any of it, the departure gets
          recorded on the measurement page.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="cta-btns">
        <Button href="/measure" variant="primary">Send one in</Button>
        <Button href="/graphs" variant="ghost">See the measurements</Button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  .mt { padding: 56px 24px 24px; }
  .crumb {
    font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11.5px; color: var(--muted-2);
    display: flex; gap: 9px; margin-bottom: 26px;
  }
  .crumb a:hover { color: var(--text); }
  .crumb [aria-current] { color: var(--muted); }
  .mt h1 {
    font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(34px, 5.2vw, 54px); line-height: 1.05; margin: 12px 0 0;
  }
  .lede {
    color: var(--muted); font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.6;
    max-width: 64ch; margin: 20px 0 0; text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .terms {
    margin-top: 40px; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--surface); overflow: hidden;
  }
  .sec .terms { margin-top: 8px; }
  .term {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: 220px 1fr; gap: 20px;
    padding: 16px 22px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); align-items: baseline;
  }
  .term:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }
  .tk {
    font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 10.5px; letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent-soft);
  }
  .tv { font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--text); }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .term { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 6px; }
  }

  .cta-row { display: flex; gap: 12px; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-top: 32px; }
  .cta-row.tight { margin-top: 20px; }

  .sec { margin-top: 52px; max-width: 68ch; }
  .sec h2 {
    font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 400; font-size: 26px; margin: 0 0 14px;
  }
  .sec p {
    color: var(--muted); font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 14px; text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .sec p.after { margin-top: 14px; }
  .sec p b { color: var(--text); font-weight: 500; }

  .ln-cta {
    margin-top: 60px;
    background: linear-gradient(120deg, rgba(255, 158, 44, 0.07), var(--surface) 55%);
    border: 1px solid var(--accent-line); border-radius: 18px; padding: 30px 32px;
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 24px; flex-wrap: wrap;
  }
  .cta-h {
    font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 400; font-size: 24px; margin: 0; max-width: 24ch;
  }
  .ln-cta p { color: var(--muted); font-size: 15px; margin: 8px 0 0; max-width: 52ch; }
  .cta-btns { display: flex; gap: 12px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
</style>
